WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.988
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.988 --> 00:00:09.810
My first time on Mars was unforgettable.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:16.099
After touring every corner of the Earth,
living in post-apocalyptic Boston and

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:20.939
studying at a dozen universities,
I thought I had seen it all.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:25.936
But well on the ground on the red planet,
driving my rover through the whipping

00:00:25.936 --> 00:00:29.890
dust, that giddy feeling of
wonder swept over me once again.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:33.852
Of course,
I wasn't actually in any of these places,

00:00:33.852 --> 00:00:37.395
but both my brain and
my body believed that I was.

00:00:37.395 --> 00:00:42.038
And at the end of the day,
is there really any difference?

00:00:42.038 --> 00:00:45.378
I'm talking, of course,
about virtual reality.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:50.939
Virtual reality has been called many
things, from a passing fad, like 3D

00:00:50.939 --> 00:00:56.862
television, to the next big computing
platform on the level of mobile phones.

00:00:56.862 --> 00:01:01.824
By now, many of us have already had
the chance to try out VR once or twice.

00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:07.408
And have already started to make
assumptions about the impact that it may,

00:01:07.408 --> 00:01:08.656
or may not have.

00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:13.530
But regardless of your stance, if I ask
you to picture who's actually using

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.201
VR today, most of us will come
up with an image like this.

00:01:17.201 --> 00:01:20.714
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:20.714 --> 00:01:21.633
&gt;&gt; That's me,

00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:26.482
someone isolated in their living room,
waving their arms furiously

00:01:26.482 --> 00:01:30.841
in the air in between sips of Soylent and
handfuls of Doritos.

00:01:30.841 --> 00:01:33.952
There's a ton of stigma about VR.

00:01:33.952 --> 00:01:38.795
People think it's for gamers or
claim that it's overhyped or

00:01:38.795 --> 00:01:44.206
fear that it's going to have
unintended consequences on humanity.

00:01:44.206 --> 00:01:49.312
And for the record,
I think these are all legitimate concerns.

00:01:49.312 --> 00:01:54.228
But looking beyond them, there is so
much more to virtual reality and

00:01:54.228 --> 00:01:56.186
the promise that it holds.

00:01:56.186 --> 00:02:00.942
I believe in the future where VR will
be used around the world to improve

00:02:00.942 --> 00:02:04.178
people's lives and
bring us closer together.

00:02:04.178 --> 00:02:07.148
But we are long way away
from that right now,

00:02:07.148 --> 00:02:11.258
which leads us to the biggest
open question facing it today.

00:02:11.258 --> 00:02:13.222
What is it really for?

00:02:13.222 --> 00:02:18.306
Or perhaps more importantly,
what can it be for?

00:02:18.306 --> 00:02:22.901
My own obsession with VR lies
at the heart of this question.

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:27.543
In my quest to answer it, I've tried
to enter VR every single day for

00:02:27.543 --> 00:02:31.627
the past few years, even if just for
a few minutes at a time.

00:02:31.627 --> 00:02:35.300
In that time, I've seen some things.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.042
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:37.042 --> 00:02:38.353
&gt;&gt; I've met people and

00:02:38.353 --> 00:02:39.980
made real connections.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.889
I've used it as a tool to improve
learning and education, and

00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:48.718
I've even built new worlds and
shared those experience with other people.

00:02:48.718 --> 00:02:53.409
But regardless of the specific
application, there is one fundamental

00:02:53.409 --> 00:02:58.190
concept that makes VR so promising to me,
and all of it's enthusiasts.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:02.482
It's its ability to create presence.

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:07.370
Presence is achieved when your brain
truly believes that you no longer just

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:12.197
standing in your bedroom or your living
room but somewhere out entirely.

00:03:12.197 --> 00:03:17.602
What VR is and will be for
is anything that requires presence.

00:03:17.602 --> 00:03:23.985
And especially when presence is either
expensive or even impossible to achieve.

00:03:23.985 --> 00:03:28.880
Consider this, we spend a tremendous
amount of time transporting our physical

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.153
brains to places that they're not.

00:03:31.153 --> 00:03:33.499
Think of multi-hour commutes or

00:03:33.499 --> 00:03:37.276
flying around the world just
to go to a meeting or two.

00:03:37.276 --> 00:03:39.245
Why do we do this?

00:03:39.245 --> 00:03:42.907
It's because being face
to face with someone,

00:03:42.907 --> 00:03:46.212
feeling present in the same room matters.

00:03:46.212 --> 00:03:50.232
Video conferencing was heralded as
the technology that was going to end

00:03:50.232 --> 00:03:51.645
the office as we know it.

00:03:51.645 --> 00:03:56.706
But it never delivered on that promise
because it couldn't achieve presence.

00:03:56.706 --> 00:04:01.689
But what if we could create VR experiences
that made you truly feel like you were in

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:03.900
that meeting, with those people?

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:10.126
How would our concept of the commute,
or work in general, change?

00:04:10.126 --> 00:04:12.528
While presence is foundational,

00:04:12.528 --> 00:04:16.597
what is even more exciting is
what we can build on top of it.

00:04:16.597 --> 00:04:19.805
The research shows that
when we're present in VR,

00:04:19.805 --> 00:04:23.460
it actually makes us feel like
things are happening to us,

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.209
as opposed to us just watching
them happen to someone else.

00:04:27.209 --> 00:04:32.622
And when we can truly believe that we
are somewhere, or even someone else, we've

00:04:32.622 --> 00:04:37.808
just entered the most powerful vehicle for
empathy that we've ever created.

00:04:37.808 --> 00:04:39.659
Now, let's pause and reflect for a moment.

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:43.034
Why did you all come here
today to watch Loki Notes,

00:04:43.034 --> 00:04:46.105
instead of just catching
it on YouTube later?

00:04:46.105 --> 00:04:48.641
It certainly can't be because of comfort.

00:04:48.641 --> 00:04:52.753
I'm sure you couch is much nicer that
these chairs and definitely standing.

00:04:52.753 --> 00:04:54.343
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:54.343 --> 00:04:56.089
&gt;&gt; On almost all metrics,

00:04:56.089 --> 00:05:00.716
the at-home experience is better
than life, except for one,

00:05:00.716 --> 00:05:05.623
we come to live events like these
because we want to feel something.

00:05:05.623 --> 00:05:09.869
To absorb the energy of the speaker,
and the people around us.

00:05:09.869 --> 00:05:11.568
To laugh, and to cry,

00:05:11.568 --> 00:05:16.319
and to share those feelings with
the people sitting next to us.

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:22.290
In these live experiences,
it's our presence that powers empathy.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:26.366
But, we can't always come to live events,
just like we can't always fly

00:05:26.366 --> 00:05:29.742
around the world to understand
how people live their lives.

00:05:29.742 --> 00:05:37.162
So to achieve presence, we usually rely
on reading stories or watching videos.

00:05:37.162 --> 00:05:40.807
But it's one thing to read
a story about Italy or

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:45.648
to see a picture of Rome or
to even watch a movie like Gladiator.

00:05:45.648 --> 00:05:51.278
But it's an entirely different thing
to step into Julius Caesar's shoes and

00:05:51.278 --> 00:05:53.016
see the world as he did.

00:05:53.016 --> 00:05:58.282
The research supports the fact that
when we have experiences even if they're

00:05:58.282 --> 00:06:03.304
just simulated ones, it makes us much
better connect with and understand

00:06:03.304 --> 00:06:08.667
that content up to four times more than if
were just to read about it or watch it.

00:06:08.667 --> 00:06:13.058
So while TV it gives us a window
to peer into another world,

00:06:13.058 --> 00:06:18.361
VR lets us step into that world and
throughly immerse ourselves in it.

00:06:18.361 --> 00:06:23.075
It's an entirely new medium for
empathetic storytelling.

00:06:23.075 --> 00:06:25.585
But what stories can we tell?

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:30.332
Consider Clouds over Sidra,
a VR experience built in partnership

00:06:30.332 --> 00:06:35.178
with the United Nations and
presented at the World Economic Forum.

00:06:35.178 --> 00:06:39.957
The mission was to take global leaders and
help them better understand

00:06:39.957 --> 00:06:44.655
what life is like for a young girl
living in a Syrian refugee camp, and

00:06:44.655 --> 00:06:47.656
inspire them to start making a difference.

00:06:47.656 --> 00:06:52.674
Or consider melting ice, a VR documentary
featuring Al Gore that transports

00:06:52.674 --> 00:06:57.936
people to the remote parts of the world
that are most affected by climate change.

00:06:57.936 --> 00:07:03.941
To give them a vivid understanding of
the real impact that it's already having.

00:07:03.941 --> 00:07:07.958
VR is also being used to
combat domestic violence.

00:07:07.958 --> 00:07:13.435
In a recent study, researchers have taken
men who've committed these acts and

00:07:13.435 --> 00:07:16.798
put them in the VR,
except in the body of a woman.

00:07:16.798 --> 00:07:20.327
The men are then subjected to
stimulated domestic violence.

00:07:20.327 --> 00:07:24.947
And it’s been proven that it actually
makes them more empathetic to the others

00:07:24.947 --> 00:07:28.458
around them and
will hopefully prevent future incidents.

00:07:28.458 --> 00:07:31.865
These are just a few of
the examples that are possible now.

00:07:31.865 --> 00:07:34.946
And everyday, we are learning more

00:07:34.946 --> 00:07:39.578
about how we can use VR as
a tool to reimagine empathy.

00:07:39.578 --> 00:07:43.369
Well, I'm clearly just a little
bit obsessed about VR.

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:45.251
I don't think that it's me or

00:07:45.251 --> 00:07:49.030
even anyone in this room who
stands the most to gain from it.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.401
VR is going to unlock experiences for

00:07:51.401 --> 00:07:57.051
the vast majority of people in this world
who don't have access to them right now.

00:07:57.051 --> 00:08:02.041
Consider the moment when anyone with
a headset can feel they're sitting in

00:08:02.041 --> 00:08:06.792
a classroom at a top university,
listening to inspiring educators and

00:08:06.792 --> 00:08:11.013
having real social interactions
with the people next to them.

00:08:11.013 --> 00:08:17.701
That's the moment when we've made
world-class education available to anyone.

00:08:17.701 --> 00:08:22.140
The release of cheap but high quality
VR headsets in the coming years will

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:26.433
make it possible for everyone,
not just to feel greater empathy, but

00:08:26.433 --> 00:08:30.827
to have experiences that were
previously impossible for them to have.

00:08:30.827 --> 00:08:35.568
So regardless of the opinion about VR,
you walked in with here today, I

00:08:35.568 --> 00:08:40.401
encourage you to broaden your perspective
on what it can and will be for.

00:08:40.401 --> 00:08:45.205
Much like how black and
white television or the first mobile phone

00:08:45.205 --> 00:08:50.288
only hinted at what was to come,
VR is still very much in its infancy.

00:08:50.288 --> 00:08:54.940
It will take time, but
it's a future worth building towards.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:59.101
And in that future,
everyone will have the ability to

00:08:59.101 --> 00:09:02.806
feel present anywhere
with anyone at any time.

00:09:02.806 --> 00:09:05.866
And the human race will be much
more connected because of it.

00:09:05.866 --> 00:09:08.292
Thank you.

00:09:08.292 --> 00:09:10.575
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:10.575 --> 00:09:14.643
[MUSIC]

